1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,280
A few people asked me what you're actually meant to do with Scout when your team's on defense or getting completely rolled or you're playing on some narrow map like Dust Bowl where there's barely anywhere to move.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,000
And they all sound like different questions, but it's pretty much the same problem.

3
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:15,160
You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore.

4
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:18,440
Because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well.

5
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:25,180
Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated, you've got space to run around and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up.

6
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,300
But the second your team loses all that pressure and every doorway starts getting watched

7
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game

8
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,560
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit using chip damage

9
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:47,220
Sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on and waiting for someone to overextend

10
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,760
Instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned

11
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:54,300
because even when an opening looks really good you still have to think

12
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:57,780
about whether your team is alive, whether anyone can actually help you and whether

13
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:00,960
dying for that play is even worth it, especially when you're defending on red

14
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,880
in payload or attack defend because you can't take your life for granted like

15
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:09,000
blue team cat because those respawn times on certain maps get just

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,440
ridiculous and one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like

17
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:17,820
forever. And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually

18
00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:21,600
shows. You can make half the enemy team turn around, pull someone away from the

19
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:26,760
objective or force their medic backwards but your team still has to notice and do

20
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,620
something with it. So we're gonna go through all of that with some

21
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:32,360
pre-recorded clips that I'm gonna lightly commentate and how to stay

22
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:37,080
useful when you can't hold W, how to know when to commit and how to tell when you

23
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,980
made a bad play versus when your team just didn't take advantage of what you

24
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:45,840
created. In downtime situations like this on defense, chip damage is sometimes all

25
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:51,720
you can really do, but it's more important than people think. It makes people look back, slows them down and forces them to look for heals.

26
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:58,080
And make sure not just when pestering people, but in general, make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any position with Scout.

27
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:03,600
It's crucial that if you get the chance to not let engineers get their sentry built in the first place. If it's level 3,

28
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:08,220
you alert your team and when your combo comes out to deal with it, help them if you can at that very moment,

29
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:13,380
but otherwise pay attention to the flanks. The engineer was on the ball here and he starts building again,

30
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:16,380
So I call it out to my soldier friend and he deals with it straight away.

31
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:24,120
The engineer is probably thinking oh my god. I'm so unlucky they keep walking out soon as I build my stuff, but it isn't luck

32
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:25,500
I'm scouting out these areas

33
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:45,460
I being an actual scout and I communicating what I say to my team at the same time I not overextending I trading these two flanks like they my post I contesting them and I cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through But I not running forward trying to take a 1v2 I just taking it slow taking my time and making sure these areas stay contested because after all we playing defense

34
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:50,180
We're not the ones attacking, you don't need to hold W as much as you might think just because you're playing Scout.

35
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:07,640
Right here I'm backed into a corner by a pyro and a scout.

36
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:11,480
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

37
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,720
I run up it, jump backwards and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward

38
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:17,060
over the pyro's head.

39
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:20,920
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed and just happens to set me up to kill

40
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,140
the scout as well.

41
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:25,640
That's one of the best skills you can have as a scout, not just seamlessly using anything

42
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:30,020
around you, but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

43
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:33,660
It's a bit like Emmet becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except you're

44
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:39,400
looking at every box, railing or tiny slope and thinking, can I use it as high ground?

45
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,1000
Basically any type of elevated platform will let you get extra height and reposition with

46
00:03:42,1000 --> 00:03:46,280
less risk.

47
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:49,800
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding

48
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,480
a boxed in last with two obvious flanks.

49
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:55,500
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

50
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:59,240
Scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention without

51
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:00,600
just throwing his life away.

52
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,600
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you and

53
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:16,880
drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

54
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:20,360
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend and the enemy

55
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:24,280
has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

56
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,600
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

57
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,620
There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially

58
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,920
when you're on defence.

59
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:37,180
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them

60
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:41,360
down and keep running time off the clock, but you need to be alive to do this.

61
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:45,360
Where blue team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through,

62
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:46,840
red is more about survivability.

63
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:57,360
The previous clip was a good example of what scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area, but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you're defending, your role changes.

64
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:16,479
Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focused in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med and clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with and

65
00:05:16,759 --> 00:05:20,499
Safely apply chip damage that forces blue to retreat or delays their push

66
00:05:20,779 --> 00:05:27,099
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without over committing and throwing away the position your team has already established

67
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:51,639
Another question I got was how you're meant to play scout and narrow maps like Dust Bowl.

68
00:05:51,639 --> 00:05:55,419
Honestly, maps like Dust Bowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice

69
00:05:55,419 --> 00:06:00,339
the most seriously. They're old, restrictive maps where the balances age pretty badly,

70
00:06:00,339 --> 00:06:07,019
especially for red. Your respawn could be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds and the points can be captured absurdly quickly.

71
00:06:07,019 --> 00:06:11,899
And because of that there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly makes Scout amazing.

72
00:06:11,899 --> 00:06:18,179
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself, which fights you take, and whether you are actually where your team needs you.

73
00:06:18,319 --> 00:06:19,879
High ground is still important,

74
00:06:19,879 --> 00:06:26,399
but only if it's actually safe. Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dust Bowl leaves you exposed to snipers and

75
00:06:26,399 --> 00:06:29,579
spam. Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point and

76
00:06:29,579 --> 00:06:32,859
and be prepared to back up once your team loses that space.

77
00:06:33,579 --> 00:06:36,499
In this clip, I move around and I help my team defend the choke,

78
